                 core3h_time - Core3H board time (DBBC3 rack)

Syntax:   core3h_time=board

Response: core3h_time/board,epoch,seconds,days,time,(nominal),difference

Settable parameters:

board:      board to read, 1 to the number of IFs specified in 'dbbc3.ctl.
            (nominal maximum 8) or 'next' (see comments).  No default. The
            previous value is not available with '*'.

Monitor only parameter:

epoch       VDIF epoch (halfYearsSince2000); the value for January 1-June 30,
            2020 should be '0'

seconds     seconds since epoch

days        full days since the start of 2000 until the epoch date
            (daysSince2000); the value for January 1-June 30, 2020 should be
            '0'

time        board date/time in format YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss

nominal     nominal VDIF epoch for board 'time', shown in parentheses

difference  board time minus FS time in seconds, positive if the board leads
            the FS

Comments:
This command samples the 'time' of Core3H 'board' at the start of the next FS
UTC second.

If the 'difference' parameter is '0' ,the board's time aligns with the FS time
at the one second level. A value of '0' should only be expected if (i) the
'computer' FS time model is being used and the computer is accurately synced
to NTP or (ii) the 'rete' model is being and has been synced to the formatter
and has an accurate rate. If one of those two conditions is met and the value
is not '0', the Core3H board probably has the wrong time.

If 'board' is specified as 'next', the board requested is one greater than the
previous board requested (wrapping back to '1' after the last board specified
by the number of IFs in 'dbbc3.ctl'). This applies regardless of whether the
previous request used 'next' or specified a board explicitly. The board for
'next' is determined by the previous request and not the previous board that
had a successful response; this prevents' 'next' from getting stuck on a board
with a problem. After a FS restart, the 'next' board is '1'.

It may take more than one second for the board to respond. As a result, the
board 'time' shown may not match the FS log time stamp. However, 'difference'
is calculated as the board time returned minus the FS time when the request
was made. If it is '0', they agree at the one second level.

Another consequence of the board sometimes taking more than one second to
respond is that consecutive 'core3h_mode=...' commands may not return board
times that are consecutive.

The '(nominal)' VDIF epoch value is not returned by the board. It is
calculated based on the board 'time' and displayed for informational purposes
in parentheses.

The VDIF epoch is described by this section from the VDIF specification:

  Reference epoch: [It is] ... the 6-month period in which the VDIF clock was
  set, with an origin of at 00UTC 1 Jan 2000, such that ‘0’ corresponds to the
  first 6 months of 2000, ‘1’ corresponds to the 6 months starting 00H 1 July
  2000, etc. After setting, [it] ... is static and is not incremented. [It]
  ... rolls over to 0 again when the reference epoch corresponds to the
  6-month period starting 00H 1 Jan 2032.

NOTE: Some DBBC3 firmware versions may automatically increment the epoch value
at the start of a new 6-month period.

All dates and times are UTC.
